{"name":"Blue Cola","hex":"#0088dc","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"cola","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The kola plant, genus Cola, famous for its nut, or one of these nuts.","A beverage or a drink made with kola nut flavoring, caramel and carbonated water."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of colon."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cola"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cola","blocks":[{"text":"Cola is a carbonated soft drink flavored with vanilla, cinnamon, citrus oils, and other flavorings. The name, however, comes from the kola nut, which imbues the beverage with the stimulant caffeine. A stimulating soda using kola appeared circa 1880 in the United States."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Glass_of_Cola.jpg/330px-Glass_of_Cola.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cola","wikidata":"Q134041"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Berry_cola_stand_Reuilly.jpg/500px-Berry_cola_stand_Reuilly.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Berry_cola_stand_Reuilly.jpg/1280px-Berry_cola_stand_Reuilly.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Berry_cola_stand_Reuilly.jpg/500px-Berry_cola_stand_Reuilly.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bottles of Berry Cola, a soft drink produced in Indre, France","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABerry_cola_stand_Reuilly.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/15-09-26-RalfR-WLC-0098_-_Coca-Cola_glass_bottle_%28Germany%29.jpg/500px-15-09-26-RalfR-WLC-0098_-_Coca-Cola_glass_bottle_%28Germany%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/15-09-26-RalfR-WLC-0098_-_Coca-Cola_glass_bottle_%28Germany%29.jpg/1280px-15-09-26-RalfR-WLC-0098_-_Coca-Cola_glass_bottle_%28Germany%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/15-09-26-RalfR-WLC-0098_-_Coca-Cola_glass_bottle_%28Germany%29.jpg/500px-15-09-26-RalfR-WLC-0098_-_Coca-Cola_glass_bottle_%28Germany%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A small glass bottle of Coca-Cola, the first cola","link":"https